      "text": "1. We need to find the largest divisor of 342 that is also a factor of 285. This involves determining the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 342 and 285.\n2. To find the GCD, we can use the Euclidean algorithm, which is an efficient method for computing the greatest common divisor of two numbers.\n3. The Euclidean algorithm is based on the principle that the GCD of two numbers also divides their difference. Thus, we repeatedly apply the division algorithm to the pair of numbers, reducing the problem to finding the GCD of smaller numbers.\n4. **Step-by-step Euclidean algorithm implementation:**\n\n   - Start with \\( a = 342 \\) and \\( b = 285 \\).\n   - Compute \\( 342 \\mod 285 \\):\n     \\[\n     342 \\div 285 = 1 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 342 - 285 \\times 1 = 57\n     \\]\n     So, \\( 342 \\mod 285 = 57 \\).\n\n   - Next, set \\( a = 285 \\) and \\( b = 57 \\):\n     \\[\n     285 \\div 57 = 5 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 285 - 57 \\times 5 = 0\n     \\]\n     So, \\( 285 \\mod 57 = 0 \\).\n\nSince the remainder is now 0, the GCD is the last non-zero remainder, which is 57.\n\nThe greatest common divisor of 342 and 285 is 57. Therefore, the largest divisor of 342 that is also a factor of 285 is \\boxed{57}.",
